# Exact versions for everything that comes from the Raspberry Pi archive.
#
# That archive publishes no snapshot service, so a suite name alone does not
# identify a build. These pins make two builds of the same commit resolve the
# same kernel and boot firmware against the archive as it stands today, and they
# make drift detectable — not impossible. apt assigns priority to versions the
# index actually offers, so once the archive supersedes a version and stops
# publishing it under the name the build installs, the pin matches nothing, the
# sole published version wins at its default priority, and the build falls
# forward in silence. The image suite is what catches that:
# tests/image/080-package-pins.test.sh compares installed versions against this
# file, and tests/image/140-package-set.test.sh compares installed names against
# the tracked manifest. Building against an archive we control, so that an old
# commit still resolves what it named, is TODO(rpi-archive-mirror).
#
# Priority 1001 is above the "never downgrade" threshold, so a newer version
# that has already slipped into a cache is rolled back rather than kept.
#
# Bumping a version here is a deliberate act: it changes what boots. Three
# steps:
#
#   1. change the version in the stanza below;
#   2. change the kernel entries in tests/image/expected-*.packages, which spell
#      the version inside the package name — tests/host/082-kernel-pin-manifest
#      holds the two files to each other, so a half-done bump fails `make check`
#      rather than a build;
#   3. `make image PROFILE=<profile>` then `make test-image PROFILE=<profile>`,
#      and review what changed.

Package: linux-image-* linux-headers-* linux-kbuild-* linux-base-*
Pin: version 1:6.18.50-1+rpt1
Pin-Priority: 1001

Package: raspi-firmware
Pin: version 1:1.20260915-1
Pin-Priority: 1001

Package: firmware-brcm80211
Pin: version 1:20260519-1~bpo13+1+rpt1
Pin-Priority: 1001
